Diamond Prices Statistical Analysis

Analyzed a dataset of 5,000 diamonds to determine whether diamond price and cut characteristics differed significantly from specific market assumptions.

Dataset:
The dataset contains 5,000 diamond records with variables including carat, cut, color, clarity, depth, table, price, and physical dimensions (x, y, z). The analysis also compares Premium and Fair cut diamonds.

Tools Used:
Microsoft Excel, including formulas, descriptive statistics, Z.TEST, one-sample proportion testing, and independent two-sample t-tests for hypothesis testing.

Key Finding:
The average diamond price was $3,862.42, which was significantly higher than the hypothesized average price of $3,750 (p = 0.0228). Premium diamonds had a significantly higher price per carat than Fair diamonds (p = 0.0003), while Fair diamonds had a significantly higher average carat size than Premium diamonds (p < 0.001). The proportion of diamonds with Premium or Ideal cuts was 66.32%, which was not significantly lower than the expected 67% (p = 0.1533).
